## 1. Pod YAML — complete field-by-field explanation

Tumhara YAML output real cluster se aaya hai — isko reference document ki tarah treat karo. Har important field ka exact meaning explain karta hoon.

```
## 1.1 metadata section

name: day5pod1          → Pod ka unique naam us namespace ke andar
namespace: default      → kis namespace mein hai
labels:
  run: day5pod1         → DEFAULT LABEL: jab tum kubectl run karte ho
                          bina --labels flag ke, Kubernetes automatically
                          ek label lagate hai: key="run", value=pod-naam

uid: 02d5e443-...       → Globally unique identifier — IMMUTABLE, kabhi
                          change nahi hota chahe pod update ho jaye.
                          Agar pod delete aur recreate ho, NAYA uid milta hai.

resourceVersion: "610"  → Har baar jab bhi ye object etcd mein update
                          hota hai, ye number badh jata hai. Optimistic
                          concurrency ke liye use hota hai — agar do
                          clients simultaneously same object update karne
                          ki koshish karein, server check karta hai ki
                          "tumhara resourceVersion match karta hai current
                          se? Nahi? To tumhara update stale hai, reject."

creationTimestamp       → Kubernetes ka har object apna creation time
                          track karta hai — debugging ke liye useful.

generation: 1           → Spec-level changes track karta hai. Har baar
                          spec change hone par ye badhta hai. Status
                          section mein "observedGeneration" se compare
                          karke pata chalta hai ki controller ne latest
                          spec process ki ya nahi.

## 1.2 spec section — "DESIRED STATE yahaan likha hai"

### imagePullPolicy (tumne flag kiya tha — yahan full answer)

imagePullPolicy: Always → Har baar pod start hone par registry se image
                          PULL karo, chahe node par already ho.

3 possible values:
- Always:     Har baar pull karo. "latest" tag wale images ke liye ya
              CI/CD pipelines mein jahan image frequently update hoti hai.
- IfNotPresent: Sirf pull karo agar node par image already nahi hai.
                Production stable images ke liye (faster pod startup).
- Never:       Kabhi pull mat karo — image already node par honi chahiye.
               Edge/air-gapped environments ke liye.

IMPORTANT GOTCHA (exam-relevant): Agar tum "nginx" likhte ho bina tag ke,
Kubernetes ise "nginx:latest" maanta hai aur imagePullPolicy AUTOMATICALLY
"Always" ho jata hai. Agar tum "nginx:1.25.0" likhte ho (specific tag),
default "IfNotPresent" hota hai. Ye default behavior interview mein pucha
jaata hai.

### Private registry se image pull karna (tumne flag kiya tha)

Steps:
1. Docker registry ka credentials se Secret banao:
   kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
     --docker-server=<registry-url> \
     --docker-username=<username> \
     --docker-password=<password> \
     --docker-email=<email>

2. Pod spec mein imagePullSecrets reference karo:
   spec:
     containers:
     - image: private-registry.io/myapp:latest
       name: myapp
     imagePullSecrets:
     - name: regcred

Kubernetes kubelet is Secret ko padh kar registry authenticate karta hai
image pull karne se pehle.

### schedulerName: default-scheduler

Tumne note kiya: "scheduler ke naam ke liye spec check karo" — sahi hai.

```yaml
spec:
  schedulerName: default-scheduler
```

CUSTOM SCHEDULER — tumne pucha tha: "Kya hum scheduler tweak kar sakte hain?"

Haan — MULTIPLE ways:

WAY 1 — Custom Scheduler deploy karna:
Tum apna khud ka scheduler binary likh sakte ho ya existing custom
schedulers use kar sakte ho (jaise scheduler-plugins project). Pod mein
`schedulerName: my-custom-scheduler` specify karte ho — wo pod sirf tab
schedule hoga jab tumhara custom scheduler running hoga.

WAY 2 — Scheduler profiles (configuration):
Default scheduler bahut configurable hai — plugins enable/disable kar
sakte ho, scoring behavior tweak kar sakte ho /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
kube-scheduler.yaml mein configuration file se.

WAY 3 — Directly: Taints/Tolerations aur Node Affinity se scheduler
ke DEFAULT DECISIONS ko influence karte ho bina scheduler replace kiye.

Interview answer: "Kubernetes allows multiple schedulers to coexist —
you can write a completely custom scheduler binary and deploy it as a
pod, then reference it by name in pod specs. Alternatively, the default
scheduler is highly configurable via scheduler profiles, letting you
enable or disable specific scoring and filtering plugins without
replacing the scheduler itself."

### securityContext: {} (tumne flag kiya — yahan full explanation)

securityContext ke 2 levels hain (important distinction):

POD-LEVEL securityContext (spec.securityContext):
  Sare containers par apply hota hai.
  
CONTAINER-LEVEL securityContext (spec.containers[].securityContext):
  Sirf us ek container par apply hota hai, pod-level override karta hai.

Common fields jo production mein use hote hain:
```yaml
securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true        # container root user se na chale (security best practice)
  runAsUser: 1000           # specific UID se container run karo
  runAsGroup: 3000          # specific GID
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true  # container ka filesystem read-only karo
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # container apne privileges escalate na kar sake
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]           # sari Linux capabilities hata do by default
    add: ["NET_ADMIN"]      # sirf zaroori capabilities add karo
```

Tumhara point sahi hai: cloud clusters mein securityContext enforce
karna security best practice hai. Agar koi attacker container mein
ghus jaye aur securityContext strict na ho:
- Root se run ho raha hai → root-level damage ho sakta hai
- Filesystem writable → malicious files likh sakta hai
- Capabilities available → network sniff kar sakta hai, kernel calls
  
CKS exam mein ye BAHUT heavily tested hai. CKA mein conceptually
important hai.

### Probes — tumne flag kiya, 3 types explain karta hoon

```yaml
livenessProbe:          # "Container ALIVE hai?" 
                        # FAIL hone par: container RESTART hota hai
                        # Use case: deadlock detect karna (app responsive
                        # nahi hai lekin crash bhi nahi hua)

readinessProbe:         # "Container TRAFFIC receive karne ke liye READY hai?"
                        # FAIL hone par: Service is pod ko ENDPOINTS se
                        # HATA deti hai (traffic nahi milta, restart nahi hota)
                        # Use case: app startup mein time lagta hai
                        # (DB connection establish hone tak traffic mat do)

startupProbe:           # "Container PEHLI BAAR SHURU hua kya?"
                        # Slow-starting apps ke liye — jab tak startupProbe
                        # pass na ho, liveness/readiness probes disabled rehte hain
                        # Use case: legacy apps jo start hone mein 2-3 min lete hain
```

Teen types of probe mechanisms:
```yaml
httpGet:    # HTTP GET request bhejo, 2xx/3xx = success
  path: /healthz
  port: 8080

tcpSocket:  # TCP connection try karo, connection open = success
  port: 8080

exec:       # Command run karo container ke andar, exit code 0 = success
  command: ["cat", "/tmp/healthy"]
```

Interview one-liner: "Liveness restarts the container on failure,
readiness removes it from the service endpoint on failure — they
solve different problems. Startup probe protects slow-starting
containers from being killed by liveness before they finish initializing."

### Pod conditions (status.conditions) — 4 types explain karte hain

Tumne note kiya: "types: Initialized, Ready, ContainerReady, PodScheduled"

```
PodScheduled:        → Scheduler ne node assign kar diya hai ✓
Initialized:         → Saare init containers successfully complete ho gaye ✓
ContainersReady:     → Saare containers individually ready hain ✓
Ready:               → Pod traffic receive kar sakta hai (readinessProbe
                        pass, init containers done, sab containers ready) ✓

Order: PodScheduled → Initialized → ContainersReady → Ready
Jab tak Ready "True" na ho, Service is Pod ko endpoints mein
ADD NAHI KARTA — ek super important fact jो CKA mein kaam aata hai.
```

### terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 30

```
Jab pod delete hota hai, Kubernetes pehle SIGTERM bhejta hai container
process ko. Process ko 30 seconds milte hain gracefully shutdown karne ke
liye (ongoing requests complete karo, connections close karo). 30 seconds
ke baad bhi process alive hai → SIGKILL → force kill.

Production best practice: apni app SIGTERM handle kare aur gracefully
shutdown kare. Nahi to requests mid-flight khatam ho jayenge.
```

### tolerations in your YAML — explain karte hain

```yaml
tolerations:
- effect: NoExecute
  key: node.kubernetes.io/not-ready
  operator: Exists
  tolerationSeconds: 300
- effect: NoExecute
  key: node.kubernetes.io/unreachable
  operator: Exists
  tolerationSeconds: 300
```

YE AUTOMATICALLY ADD HOTE HAIN tumhare har pod mein (system-added
default tolerations). Meaning:
- Agar node "not-ready" ya "unreachable" ho jaye, pod 300 seconds
  (5 minutes) tak wait karega before eviction — taaki temporary
  network blip par immediately pods evict na ho jayein.
- Tum is value ko change kar sakte ho (zyada fast eviction chahiye
  to 30s, zyada patient rehna hai to zyada).

### serviceAccount: default

Har pod ek ServiceAccount ke naam se run karta hai. Default ServiceAccount
automatically every pod ko mount hota hai ek token ke roop mein:
`/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token`

Ye token pod ko api-server se baat karne ki permission deta hai (limited
RBAC permissions ke saath). Production mein ye default token often
`automountServiceAccountToken: false` karke disable karte hain kyunki
zyada zaroori nahi hota aur attack surface reduce hota hai.

### QoS Class: BestEffort (status mein)

Kubernetes teen QoS classes define karta hai resource management ke liye:

BestEffort: Koi resources.requests ya resources.limits specify nahi kiya
  → Sabse PEHLE kill hoga agar node par memory pressure aaye

Burstable: requests specify ki hain lekin limits se kam
  → Middle priority

Guaranteed: requests == limits (dono equal aur dono specified)
  → LAST MEIN kill hoga, highest priority

Interview: "QoS class determines eviction priority under node memory
pressure — Guaranteed pods are last to be evicted, BestEffort first.
It's automatically assigned by Kubernetes based on whether you set
resource requests and limits."

### restartCount

Tumne sahi note kiya: "kitni baar pod down aur up hua."
`restartCount: 0` — ye pod kabhi crash nahi hua.
High restartCount = CrashLoopBackOff pattern → `kubectl logs --previous`
se last crash ka log dekho.

### hostIP vs podIP

```
hostIP: 172.19.0.2   → Node ka IP (physical machine)
podIP:  10.244.0.5   → Pod ka IP (virtual, CNI se assigned)
```

Ye exactly Lecture 3 wala concept confirm karta hai — Pod ka IP aur
Node ka IP ALAG ALAG hote hain, do different networks.
```

